## Why This Is Asked
Assesses hands-on windowed aggregation, per-key state management, late-data handling, and correct offset commits in a beginner KCNA setup.

## Key Concepts
- Tumbling windows, per-key state, late data handling, TTL, idempotent emits, offset management.

## Code Example
```javascript
class WindowedAggSink {
  constructor(windowMs, latenessMs) {
    this.windowMs = windowMs;
    this.latenessMs = latenessMs;
    this.state = new Map(); // windowStart -> deviceId -> sum
  }
  onMessage(msg) {
    // msg: { deviceId, value, timestamp, offset }
    const ws = this.windowStartFor(msg.timestamp);
    const bucket = (this.state.get(ws) = this.state.get(ws) || new Map());
    bucket.set(msg.deviceId, (bucket.get(msg.deviceId) || 0) + msg.value);
  }
  onWindowBoundary(now) {
    // emit and clean up windows that ended before now - latenessMx
  }
  commitOffsets() {
    // persist offsets after successful emits
  }
  getTotals() {
    // return current in-memory totals for inspection
  }
  windowStartFor(ts) {
    return Math.floor(ts / this.windowMs) * this.windowMs;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design runtime SLA guarantees under multi-tenant load, focusing on scheduling, backpressure, and cross-tenant fairness in data delivery to external sinks like Snowflake.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant scheduling and backpressure
- SLA-driven latency budgeting
- Memory- and time-bounded queues
- Canary and burst testing in production-like loads

## Code Example

```javascript
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(rate, burst) {
    this.rate = rate; // tokens per second
    this.burst = burst; // max tokens in bucket
    this.tokens = burst;
    this.last = Date.now();
  }
  consume(n) {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = (now - this.last) / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.burst, this.tokens + elapsed * this.rate);
    this.last = now;
    if (this.tokens >= n) {
      this.tokens -= n;
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }
}
```

